<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/the-water-cycle">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The water cycle is <strong>a fixed planetary throughput distributing unevenly</strong> —
    the total is constant, but where and when it lands is not. Every water problem (drought,
    flood, depletion, pollution) is a mismatch between the cycle\'s geography and ours.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the inventory, the fluxes, the residence times, the scarcity" widget="equationStep" config={waterCfg} />

  <h2>Findings that reframe the problem</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Groundwater is the hidden crisis:</strong> aquifers supply half of drinking water and 40% of irrigation; the Ogallala (US), North China Plain and Indo-Gangetic basins are depleting measurably — GRACE satellites weighed the losses from orbit (NASA, 2002–).</li>
    <li><strong>Atmospheric rivers and "virtual water":</strong> a single atmospheric river can carry more water than the Mississippi; a tonne of wheat embodies ~1,300 tonnes of water — trade moves water invisibly between countries.</li>
    <li><strong>Climate change loads the dice:</strong> warmer air holds ~7% more water per °C — wet events wetter, dry spells longer (the monsoon lesson\'s sharpened extremes).</li>
    <li><strong>Solutions are known, deployment is the challenge:</strong> drip irrigation, reuse, pricing, watershed restoration, and "day zero" management (Cape Town 2018 nearly ran out — behaviour change cut use in time).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> USGS Water Science School; Mekonnen & Hoekstra, Science Advances (2016); GRACE mission data; FAO AQUASTAT.</p>
</TopicPage>
